it's too I've been thinking lately about  the question of who is religious I mean  we all have this idea of what a  religious person looks like or what he  or she acts like but is it really that  simple sure religious is just a word  that we've invented and so however we  define that word also defines what a  religious person is but the problem is  that people define this word very  differently and thus there is a lot of  misunderstanding and confusion about  this when does a person become religious  like where's the line drawn is there a  scale and if so what does that scale  look like if you've seen my previous  video on religion you'll know that there  is no official definition for that word  and needless to say the same applies for  the word religious as well we don't like  to use that word academically at all  because of how problematic it can be let  me think about this we like to consider  Saudi Arabia a very religious country  right I mean it's a Muslim country the  laws of restrict the women are usually  veiled and so on but in Saudi Arabia  really a very religious country or does  sorry but just follow a more strict  interpretation of Islam I mean there's a  difference there see this is where the  problem often arrives here in the West  where we consider ourselves to be very  secularized we often associate more  religion with more strict right so the  more religious a person is the more  strict he is or the more religious a  person is the less open-minded he is to  put it in other words more religion  equals bad and less religion equals good  but isn't this just our Western  secularized view that religion is  somehow opposed to the ideals of modern  society that that religion is associated  with all the ideas and something that  represents a bygone world are we still  really feeling the effects of the  Enlightenment when religion and all its  expressions were considered to be in  opposition to everything we wanted to  achieve as a society I mean is it really  true that if you are more religious than  you are necessarily more strict or more  rigid let's take an example so let's say  there are two women and  both of these women are Christians so  they grew up in a Christian family they  live in a Christian country and so on  the first woman let's call her Linda she  lives a rather strict life she doesn't  go out much she doesn't drink any  alcohol she dresses very modestly and  she always wears a crucifix and all of  this because she thinks that's what's  appropriate for a Christian woman the  second woman and her name will be with  Sarah she doesn't do any of these things  she goes out she drinks alcohol she  dresses and acts like any other person  on the street but every night before she  goes to bed she prays for 15 minutes she  always goes to church on Sundays and  when she's feeling down she reads the  Bible for comfort now which of these two  women would you say is more religious  let's take another example two Muslim  guys one is this stereotypical Muslim  dude like he has this he wears the long  white Muslim garb he has a long beard he  doesn't think women should be able to  drive because Islam says so he thinks  music is forbidden because Islam says so  he's very keen to follow the Sharia and  he encourages others to do so as well  but he doesn't always pray five times a  day and he has never really read the  Quran and it's entirely now the other  guy he lives in Germany he dresses in  western clothing he is a feminist and  sometimes on weekends he plays at the  local clubs with his metal band but he  never misses a prayer and he does dhikr  one time a week and he's always thinking  about God now which of these two men  would you say is more religious or more  Muslim here we're kind of getting into  the territory of what religion actually  is we discussed this briefly in a  previous episode about religion and  explained that religion can mean very  different things to different people and  people have different ideas about what  the core of religion is so for example  if you think religiosity is mainly a  personal belief or faith then you adhere  to every Protestant Christian idea of  religion and religiosity well other  cultures or regions of the world may  sort of emphasize the more external  aspects like family or behavior or law  or politics or whatever to be  the core of religion and religiosity we  often hear about a kind of identity  crisis that appear in minority  communities as a result of this kind of  understanding so a Muslim woman for  example may often feel that she has to  hide her religiosity in order to fit  into society people have a tendency to  believe that you are more Muslim if you  wear a hijab for example and this  creates a kind of dichotomy between  Islam and Western culture where the  former is seen as being in opposition to  the latter the more a person goes  against the norms of Western society the  more Muslim they are in this case and  this isn't necessarily true at all but  this kind of dichotomy sort of often  leads to things like bigotry and  ultimately racism as well but this leads  me to another related subject like  anyone with good taste in music I really  love the Beatles it's a group of just  really great songwriters and one really  good drummer but it's no secret that  their lead guitarist George Harrison  throughout his life was very much  interested in into Hinduism there are  Beatles songs that like within you  without you that clearly express  philosophies related to Vedanta and  things like that the time will come when  you see we're all one and life flows on  within you and without you and so on  especially after the Beatles in his solo  material this could be seen is album  living in the material world is almost  exclusively made up of devotional songs  to the divine but there are lot of  different opinions about this not just  if people like or don't like this  direction he took but also what it means  and what it actually is that he's doing  well I personally wouldn't hesitate to  call George Harrison a very religious  person there are a lot of people who say  that he now is just philosophical he's  just spiritual but what's the difference  here really there seems to be this idea  in the West that Eastern religions like  Hinduism or Buddhism are often more  spiritualities than they are religions  than people practicing it are more  spiritual than religious one reason for  this could be that these religions like  Buddhism when they are imported to the  west and a Western market are often D  religion eyes that is main aspects of  those religions that we would consider  religious  I've been removed from them when they  are imported to the Western market so  the kind of Buddhism practiced by a  Swedish convert may be completely  different from one practiced by an  Indian or someone in China another fact  it could be that we here in the West so  strongly associate the word religion  with the judeo-christian Islamic  framework and so this becomes the  framework by which all other religions  are measured as well in their sort of  scale of religiosity or scale of how  much of a religion they are I mean yeah  George Harrison songs are very  philosophical but those philosophies are  usually directly linked to religious  teachings within Hinduism and so doesn't  that make them religious this apparent  dichotomy between religious and  spiritual is very interesting a lot of  people claim that religion is indeed  going away in the West  church attendance is getting lower and  so on but it's actually a lot more  complicated than this it is true that  people in Sweden for example have been  moving away from Christian dogmas in  their institutionalized forms but does  this necessarily make them less  religious in a book that came out three  years ago historian of religions David  2dl writes the following in terms of  faith and philosophy Swedes have gone  through a change away from  institutionalized Christianity and its  dogmas but they have not abandoned a  religious way of approaching existence  actually some research have shown that  things like belief in an afterlife have  actually increased and the number of  people who don't believe in a God or  supernatural power or force decreased  between the years 1981 and 1997 this is  all in Sweden by the way Sweden also  ranks second in all of Europe when it  comes to number of people believing in  some sort of spirit or life force and  that God is something that lives within  us rather than outside  in spite of commonly being considered  one of the most secularized countries in  the world this is perhaps best  exemplified when showed if you ask  someone if they are religious or what  they believe in especially in Sweden it  is very likely that you will get an  answer like wow I'm spiritual but not  religious or I believe in something but  maybe not the God of religions and so on  this is a very common answer but in  certain situations like a crisis  or a national tragedy or things like  this we can clearly see that people  start sort of falling back on their  traditional religious values and  practices in this case the Christian  tradition so it's a very complicated  situation let's say it seems that if we  define religious as specific adherence  to a institutionalized religion and its  theology then indeed it appears that  it's decreasing in a way but if we  define the word more broadly than it  certainly is not that simple people  clearly still believe in things that  appear irrational or sometimes  supernatural we can see this in for  example the emergence of the New Age  movements or they're in sort of renewed  interest in Eastern religions that  aren't considered religious in the same  way what do you think  what does religious actually mean who is  religious and is there a difference  between spiritual and religious leave a  comment and we can continue this  discussion there and I'll see you next  time bye